#* {{quote-book|en|year=2007|author=Stewart Motha|chapter=Reconciliation as Domination|editor=Scott Veitch|title=Law and the Politics of Reconciliation|publisher=Routledge|year_published=2016|page=83|url=https://books.google.ca/books?id=jeQGDAAAQBAJ&printsec=frontcover#v=onepage&q&f=false
|passage=Violence is apparently introduced early by the practice of "'''cruelling'''": children even in their first months are physically punished and then encouraged to seek retribution by punishing the punisher.}}
#* '''2009''', Mark Colvin, ABC, "Peter Sutton discusses the politics of suffering in Aboriginal communities," 2 July, 2009, [http://www.abc.net.au/pm/content/2008/s2615274.htm]
#*: {{...}} I was referring to the area where you were talking about this practice of '''cruelling'''; the pinching of babies, sometimes so hard that their skin breaks and may go septic.

